feat(server): land M5 operator surface (clap subcommands + arc-swap hot reload + SIGHUP)

* `corx` binary gains four explicit subcommands while preserving the
  bare `corx` invocation:
  - `serve` (default) starts the listener;
  - `check` validates the configuration without binding and exits
    non-zero on failure -- usable as a Kubernetes init container or
    CI gate;
  - `dump --format toml|json` prints the fully-resolved configuration
    so deployments can be diffed against running processes;
  - `version` prints version + os/arch + active feature set.
  `--config` is now a global flag so it composes with every subcommand.
* New `LivePolicies` snapshot bundles every hot-swappable component
  (config, CORS, request/response filters, request guard, upstream
  client) and lives behind an `ArcSwap`. Each request loads the
  snapshot exactly once at the top of the handler chain so the policy
  view is internally consistent even if a reload races mid-request.
* Frozen-at-startup fields (server.bind / http2 / tls, body and timeout
  limits baked into the router, metrics endpoint path) move to dedicated
  `immutable_*` fields on `ServerBuild` so reloads can detect and reject
  attempts to change them.
* New `corx_server::hot_reload` module:
  - `ReloadHandle` validates immutability, recompiles a `LivePolicies`,
    and atomically swaps it via `ArcSwap::store`;
  - `watch_sighup` (Unix only) drives the handle from `SIGHUP`;
  - rejects are surfaced as `corx_config_reload_total{result="rejected"}`
    while successful reloads bump `result="ok"` and I/O errors bump
    `result="error"`.
* Five hot_reload unit tests cover identical-config success, rejected
  bind / request_timeout changes, accepted mutable-field changes, and
  field-wise TLS comparison.
* `serve()` spawns the SIGHUP watcher as a background tokio task on
  Unix; the no-op stub keeps Windows builds clean.

73 unit tests passing across the workspace under both the default and
`--all-features` builds.

BREAKING CHANGE: `ServerBuild` no longer exposes `cors`, `request_filter`,
`response_filter`, `guard`, `upstream` or `config` directly. Consumers must
now call `ServerBuild::policies()` to obtain an atomic snapshot, or read
listener-level metadata from the `immutable_server` / `immutable_limits` /
`immutable_metrics_endpoint` fields. `clap::Parser` callers must handle the
new `Command` enum -- the bare `corx` invocation still defaults to `serve`.
